'truck':{
 title:'The Truck',one:'Bigger than everything else on the road — the rumble is low, and respect for big machines starts here.',
 seoTitle:'Truck Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Vehicles and Sounds (Class 12)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: rumble like a big truck, guess what the trailer carries, and give the road’s largest visitor its proper name.',
 intro:'This lesson gives the road’s biggest regular visitor its due: the rumble, the trailer, the guessing game about what it carries — all from the safety of the window.',
 what:'A truck is a big vehicle with a trailer that goes rumble — a low rolling noise toddlers can feel in their chests when one passes close.',
 mean:'Inside this card waits a toddler’s first two-word phrase: big truck. Point, pause, and say it slowly once — the pause is where the phrase assembles itself.',
 know:'Trucks carry things — bricks, sand, food, mysterious crates. The guessing game has no wrong answers at two, and every guess is a chance to talk.',
 why:'Trucks pass less often than cars but hit harder when they do: the rarity and the rumble make each sighting an event worth naming out loud.',
 dev:'Size vocabulary (big, biggest), imitation of a low sustained sound, and early guessing-and-telling — prediction talk in its first clothes.',
 vocab:'truck, rumble, big, trailer, carry, load',
 teach:'1) Show the card and say truck, then rumble — a low chesty noise, slower and deeper than the car’s vroom.\n2) Rumble together while pushing a toy truck slowly across the rug: big trucks take up space and take their time.\n3) Hunt trucks in books and on the road from the window; when a real one passes, guess together what the trailer might be carrying.\n4) When your toddler delivers a brrr or a deep mmm, accept it as the rumble and model the full version once, unhurried.\n5) Name every real truck this week — big truck, slow rumble — and let the phrase grow one word at a time.',
 say:'"A truck! Rumble, rumble — so big. Can you make the rumble? Down deep, like this. What is the truck carrying today? Bricks? Sand? Let us watch and guess."',
 notice:'Find the label Truck first, then the word Rumble underneath. Ask which part rolls along at the back — the trailer hitched behind is the detail to spot.',
 ask:'"Where is the truck?" "What does the truck say?" "Can you rumble like a big truck?" "What do you think is riding in the trailer?"',
 childSay:'A chest-deep "rrrumble" or a pushed-out mmm — both are the truck voice arriving. Expect big truck to be applied to every large vehicle this week; the sorting refines itself later.',
 real:'Building sites, delivery mornings and motorway bridges are truck country. The pavements outside a building site are the premium seats — watch from behind the barriers, rumble on cue.',
 play:'Loading bay: fill a laundry basket with soft toys, let your toddler be the loader, then push the load across the room and tip it at the delivery point. Rumble mandatory, tipping ecstatic.',
 check:'Ask what the truck says and whether it is big or little. A rumble plus big passes. Watch for the two-word phrase — big truck — arriving in real traffic this week.',
 tips:'Say the phrase slowly with a beat between the words: big… truck. Two-word phrases are built in pauses. The guessing game needs zero props and fills a supermarket queue.',
 safety:'Trucks have huge blind spots and long stopping distances — hold hands tight at any roadside, and watch them only from behind a fence, barrier or window, never from the kerb’s edge.'},
'fire-truck':{
 title:'The Fire Truck',one:'Red, loud and heroic — nee-naw is the easiest satisfying siren a toddler mouth can make.',
 seoTitle:'Fire Truck Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Vehicles and Sounds (Class 12)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: nee-naw to the rescue, park the hero truck after the job, and make loud sirens familiar instead of frightening.',
 intro:'This lesson turns the loudest hero on the road into a rescue game: nee-naw around the rug, put the fire out, park the truck — with real sirens made familiar instead of scary.',
 what:'A fire truck is a big red rescue vehicle with a ladder and a siren that goes nee-naw — the sound toddlers master fastest of all the emergency noises.',
 mean:'Nee-naw is a sliding two-note sound: pitch going up and down in one word. Sliding pitch is ear-training for the melody of speech, smuggled inside a siren.',
 know:'Fire trucks go out when there is a fire and help people — say that plainly. If real sirens worry your toddler, playing the sound on purpose shrinks it back down to size.',
 why:'This is the deck’s drama card. The rescue game gives big feelings a storyline with an ending — fire out, everybody safe, truck parked — which is exactly how toddlers like their stories.',
 dev:'Pitch sliding and imitation, pretend play with a clear beginning and end, and the first idea of helpers — people whose job is to come when things go wrong.',
 vocab:'fire truck, nee-naw, ladder, red, rescue, hero',
 teach:'1) Show the card and say fire truck, then nee-naw — let the sound slide up and down like the real siren.\n2) Push a cushion-fire-truck around the room together, nee-nawing at full volume until the pretend fire is out.\n3) Hunt fire trucks in books and toy boxes; find the ladder on each one and name it every time.\n4) When the siren comes out as a shriek or a whisper, take either — the slide matters more than the volume, and both are practice.\n5) Give rescues their slot this week: one fire, one rescue, one proud parking at the end, and the game closes itself neatly.',
 say:'"Fire truck! Nee-naw, nee-naw! Make the siren sound with me — up and down, nee-naw. The fire truck is red with a big ladder, and it drives fast to help. Rescue done — park the truck!"',
 notice:'The card says Fire Truck at the top with Nee-naw written beneath. Hunt for the siren words, then hunt for the ladder riding on top.',
 ask:'"Where is the fire truck?" "What does the fire truck say?" "Can you slide your nee-naw?" "What does the fire truck do after the fire is out?"',
 childSay:'"NEE-NAW!" at whatever volume the day allows, or a sliding siren with no words at all. Some toddlers park their cushion trucks and announce done! — the ending is part of the story they learned.',
 real:'Real sirens pass on real streets. Name them calmly — "fire truck, nee-naw, going to help" — and the sound becomes information instead of alarm. Fire stations sometimes let small visitors sit in the truck; worth a call.',
 play:'Rescue the teddy: a bear is stuck on the sofa mountain, the fire truck rushes over, the ladder (your arm) extends, the bear is saved, the truck parks. A story with an ending, every single time.',
 check:'Ask what the fire truck says and what colour it is. A nee-naw plus red passes. The deeper pass is the calm one — a real siren outside that gets a nod instead of a wobble.',
 tips:'Always finish the rescue and park the truck; the ending is what makes the game repeatable without escalation. When a real siren startles, narrate first and reassure second — information calms better than shushing.',
 safety:'Real fire trucks mean move aside and let them pass — teach that with a step to the side and a hand held. At home, cushion rescues stay on the floor; no climbing the actual furniture for the sake of a teddy.'},
